desc=f"""<p><strong>DRAFT, version 1.0.</strong> A methods note on the <em>Truth Gate</em>, a mechanical check that binds a Lean 4 formalization to the exact claim its paper makes.</p>
<p>Each formalization repository carries <code>Semantics.lean</code> with <code>formal_claim</code> (the statement of the main Lean theorem) and <code>paper_claim</code>, an independent Lean transcription of a verbatim sentence of the paper, recorded with the paper's path, its SHA-256 and its section. <code>paper_claim</code> is never defined as, unfolded to or aliased to <code>formal_claim</code>. The theorem <code>intended_correspondence : formal_claim &harr; paper_claim</code> must be proved by real reasoning (no <code>rfl</code>/<code>Iff.rfl</code>), with no <code>sorry</code>, <code>admit</code> or <code>native_decide</code>, and only the axioms <code>propext</code>, <code>Classical.choice</code> and <code>Quot.sound</code>. CI fails on drift, and the lock must carry the author's approval.</p>
<p>The reusable checker (BenFrohman/PipelineHalt PR #4, merge commit 56e29ecaf9b1616eaa327543d7e357d8f5d86fd8) rejects aliasing, rfl correspondences, sorry, admit, native_decide, nonstandard axioms, missing provenance and failed builds. Its CI run 43993050555 on that commit reports 15/15 locked-and-approved negative fixtures rejected and 6/6 post-approval tamper cases correct (one passing demo, five rejections).</p>
<p><strong>No partial locks and reporting solved problems.</strong> The note states the author's no-partial-locks rule (a lock certifies only a kernel-proved step on the correct path; drift and stand-in types or names are rejected) and his rule of 16 November 2026, 7:49 PM CT, quoted verbatim: &ldquo;Always tell user immediately when an OPEN problem is SOLVED regardless if it is the open problem i am working on or another that happens to be resolved by proxy of nearby findings or by direct aim or by side tangental work that brings about other findings.&rdquo; &ldquo;Solved&rdquo; means honestly proved: kernel-checked under every gate rule (independent paper_claim, no aliasing or rfl, standard axioms only, no sorry, no partial locks). A lock certifies that the Lean matches the paper's locked claim; whether that claim settles an open problem depends on what it states, and conditional locks stay conditional. No lock in this note states that an open problem is solved.</p>
<p><strong>Re-audit.</strong> On 17 November 2026 (UTC) a re-audit under the no-partial-locks rule reopened 11 merged locks as draft pull requests, gate red on purpose: {reopen_html}. The note's case study is the frohmanian-tether-verified lock (merged at 9acd434, hardened at fa82d34): it passed every mechanical check and was reopened because its main theorem restates its own hypothesis (a tautology), the quoted sentence is a refuted-claims item rather than a step, and a type name is a stand-in. This shows what the mechanical checks do not detect. The frohmanian-tether-sdiff lock (56b540c) is among the reopened ones. The eth-conflict-clause-lattice lock (797f029) has no reopen PR, but no published verdict of the re-audit on it was found, so the note gives no confirmed sound example. Two drift items reported red in NS_Millennium_Proof PR #34 (open) are also described.</p>
<p><strong>Reproducibility.</strong> https://github.com/BenFrohman/PipelineHalt at commit 56e29ecaf9b1616eaa327543d7e357d8f5d86fd8; fixture toolchain leanprover/lean4:v4.34.0 (core Lean, no Mathlib). Verify: <code>git clone https://github.com/BenFrohman/PipelineHalt &amp;&amp; cd PipelineHalt &amp;&amp; git checkout 56e29ecaf9b1616eaa327543d7e357d8f5d86fd8 &amp;&amp; npm install &amp;&amp; npx tsc -b &amp;&amp; cd test/fixtures/truth-gate-lean &amp;&amp; lake build &amp;&amp; ./run-negatives.sh &amp;&amp; ./run-tamper.sh</code>. The case study is at frohmanian-tether-verified fa82d34a15a0c61d2ba24167517896ab5276c52d (Lean v4.30.0-rc1, Mathlib 0c154d67103f74be3a0f2c509f72ccbf5be9f2a7; axioms [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]).</p>
<p>&copy; 2026 Benjamin Stanley Frohman. Licensed under CC BY 4.0.</p>"""
